\section*{Annex 8: Reconnaissance Microservice -- Python Code Listing}

The following listing shows the core \texttt{ReconService} class that orchestrates the five reconnaissance tools. The class is structured around a single \texttt{run()} entry-point that dispatches to per-tool service classes, aggregates their results, and projects them onto the knowledge graph via the \texttt{GraphBuilder}.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=python]
from typing import Any, Dict
from flask import Flask, request, jsonify
from .tools.nmap import NmapService
from .tools.nuclei import NucleiService
from .tools.gobuster import GobusterService
from .tools.subfinder import SubfinderService
from .tools.wpscan import WPScanService
from .graph import GraphBuilder
from .parsers import ResultParser

app = Flask(__name__)

TOOLS = {
    "nmap":     NmapService,
    "nuclei":   NucleiService,
    "gobuster": GobusterService,
    "subfinder": SubfinderService,
    "wpscan":   WPScanService,
}

@app.route("/recon/scan", methods=["POST"])
def run_scan():
    payload = request.get_json(force=True)
    target  = payload["target"]
    tools   = payload.get("tools", list(TOOLS.keys()))
    profile = payload.get("profile", "balanced")
    jitter  = payload.get("jitter_ms", 100)

    builder = GraphBuilder()
    results: Dict[str, Any] = {}
    for tool_name in tools:
        svc = TOOLS[tool_name](profile=profile, jitter_ms=jitter)
        raw = svc.run(target)
        findings = ResultParser(tool_name).parse(raw)
        results[tool_name] = findings
        builder.project(tool_name, target, findings)

    builder.compute_blast_radius()
    return jsonify({"target": target, "results": results,
                    "graph": builder.to_cytoscape()})
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 9: AI Microservice -- Strict JSON Schema Response Example}

The AI microservice enforces a strict JSON schema on every response produced by the local Ollama (\texttt{qwen2.5-coder:7b}) model. The four mandatory top-level keys are \texttt{risk\_summary}, \texttt{key\_findings}, \texttt{remediation\_as\_code}, and \texttt{citations}; the \texttt{citations} array must reference exact line numbers in the raw tool output, which is the primary anti-hallucination control.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=json]
{
  "risk_summary": "ensi.tn exposes 4 open ports (22, 80, 443, 8080) and 2 critical CVEs were detected by Nuclei. Apache OFBiz Path Traversal (CVE-2024-1234) and WordPress Plugin SQL Injection (CVE-2023-5678) both allow unauthenticated remote code execution.",
  "key_findings": [
    {
      "id": "F-001",
      "severity": "critical",
      "title": "Apache OFBiz Path Traversal RCE",
      "cve": "CVE-2024-1234",
      "cvss": 9.8,
      "matched_at": "https://ensi.tn/webtools/control/ProgramExport;/~Example"
    },
    {
      "id": "F-002",
      "severity": "high",
      "title": "Git Config Exposure",
      "matched_at": "https://ensi.tn/.git/config"
    }
  ],
  "remediation_as_code": [
    {
      "language": "bash",
      "title": "Block /webtools/control/ProgramExport at the reverse proxy",
      "script": "#!/bin/bash\nlocation /webtools/control/ProgramExport { return 403; }"
    },
    {
      "language": "ansible",
      "title": "Remove .git directory from document root",
      "script": "- name: Remove exposed .git directory\n  file:\n    path: /var/www/html/.git\n    state: absent"
    }
  ],
  "citations": [
    {"finding_id": "F-001", "source": "nuclei", "raw_line": 14, "evidence": "CVE-2024-1234 matched at /webtools/control/ProgramExport"},
    {"finding_id": "F-002", "source": "nuclei", "raw_line": 22, "evidence": "git-config template matched at /.git/config"}
  ]
}
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 10: Remediation-as-Code -- Bash Script Example}

The following Bash remediation script was generated by the AI microservice for the Git Config Exposure finding. The script is self-contained, idempotent, and logs every action it takes.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=bash]
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Remediation: Git Config Exposure on ensi.tn
# Finding ID: F-002
# Generated by: AI Microservice (qwen2.5-coder:7b)
# Idempotent: yes -- safe to re-run

set -euo pipefail
IFS=$'\n\t'

DOC_ROOT="${1:-/var/www/html}"
LOG_FILE="/var/log/remediation/F-002-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).log"
mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LOG_FILE")"

log() { echo "[$(date -Iseconds)] $*" | tee -a "$LOG_FILE"; }

log "Starting remediation for F-002 (Git Config Exposure)"

if [ -d "$DOC_ROOT/.git" ]; then
    log "Found .git directory at $DOC_ROOT/.git -- removing"
    rm -rf "$DOC_ROOT/.git"
    log "Removed $DOC_ROOT/.git"
else
    log "No .git directory at $DOC_ROOT/.git -- nothing to do"
fi

# Add nginx rule to block any future .git exposure
NGINX_CONF="/etc/nginx/snippets/block-git.conf"
if [ ! -f "$NGINX_CONF" ]; then
    log "Creating nginx snippet at $NGINX_CONF"
    cat > "$NGINX_CONF" <<'EOF'
location ~ /\.git {
    deny all;
    return 404;
}
EOF
    log "Reload nginx to apply the new snippet"
    nginx -t && systemctl reload nginx
fi

log "Remediation F-002 complete"
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 11: Remediation-as-Code -- Ansible Playbook Example}

The same Git Config Exposure remediation expressed as an Ansible playbook. The playbook is the preferred format when the customer's operations team already runs Ansible.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=ansible]
---
# Remediation: Git Config Exposure on ensi.tn
# Finding ID: F-002
# Generated by: AI Microservice (qwen2.5-coder:7b)

- name: Remove exposed .git directory and block future exposure
  hosts: webservers
  become: yes
  vars:
    doc_root: /var/www/html
    nginx_snippet: /etc/nginx/snippets/block-git.conf

  tasks:
    - name: Ensure .git directory is absent from document root
      ansible.builtin.file:
        path: "{{ doc_root }}/.git"
        state: absent
      register: git_removed

    - name: Log .git removal
      ansible.builtin.debug:
        msg: "Removed {{ doc_root }}/.git"
      when: git_removed.changed

    - name: Ensure nginx snippet directory exists
      ansible.builtin.file:
        path: "{{ nginx_snippet | dirname }}"
        state: directory
        mode: '0755'

    - name: Deploy nginx snippet to block .git exposure
      ansible.builtin.copy:
        dest: "{{ nginx_snippet }}"
        mode: '0644'
        content: |
          location ~ /\.git {
              deny all;
              return 404;
          }
      notify: Reload nginx

  handlers:
    - name: Reload nginx
      ansible.builtin.systemd:
        name: nginx
        state: reloaded
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 12: Remediation-as-Code -- Dockerfile Example}

For findings that require rebuilding a container image (e.g.\ upgrading a vulnerable base image), the AI microservice produces a Dockerfile patch as shown below.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=dockerfile]
# Remediation: CVE-2023-5678 -- WordPress Plugin SQL Injection
# Finding ID: F-003
# Generated by: AI Microservice (qwen2.5-coder:7b)
# Base: wordpress:6.5-php8.2-apache
# Patched: wordpress:6.5-php8.2-apache-patched

FROM wordpress:6.5-php8.2-apache

# Disable the vulnerable plugin (example-plugin <= 1.4.2)
RUN rm -rf /var/www/html/wp-content/plugins/example-plugin/

# Install the Wordfence CLI and scan the plugins directory
RUN curl -sL https://github.com/wordfence/wordfence-cli/releases/latest/download/wordfence-linux-amd64 -o /usr/local/bin/wordfence \
    && chmod +x /usr/local/bin/wordfence \
    && /usr/local/bin/wordfence malware-scan /var/www/html/wp-content/plugins/ --no-interactive

# Re-enable the patched version of the plugin
COPY example-plugin-1.5.0.zip /tmp/
RUN unzip /tmp/example-plugin-1.5.0.zip -d /var/www/html/wp-content/plugins/ \
    && rm /tmp/example-plugin-1.5.0.zip

# Drop privileges
USER www-data

# Healthcheck
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --retries=3 \
    CMD curl -f http://localhost/ || exit 1
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 13: docker-compose.yml Excerpt}

The following excerpt from the production \texttt{docker-compose.yml} highlights the security hardening applied to every service (non-root user, read-only root filesystem, all capabilities dropped, no-new-privileges) and the two isolated networks.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=yaml]
version: "3.9"

services:
  laravel:
    image: ghcr.io/aymenazizi/cybersec-laravel:signed
    user: "1001:1001"
    read_only: true
    cap_drop: [ALL]
    security_opt: [no-new-privileges:true]
    tmpfs: [/tmp, /var/run]
    environment:
      - APP_ENV=production
      - DB_HOST=postgres
      - REDIS_HOST=redis
    networks: [internal]
    depends_on:
      postgres: {condition: service_healthy}
      redis:    {condition: service_started}
    restart: unless-stopped

  recon:
    image: ghcr.io/aymenazizi/cybersec-recon:signed
    user: "1001:1001"
    read_only: true
    cap_drop: [ALL]
    security_opt: [no-new-privileges:true]
    tmpfs: [/tmp]
    networks: [internal]
    restart: unless-stopped

  postgres:
    image: postgres:16-age
    user: postgres
    environment:
      - POSTGRES_DB=cybersec
      - POSTGRES_PASSWORD_FILE=/run/secrets/pg_password
    volumes:
      - pg-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    secrets: [pg_password]
    networks: [internal]
    healthcheck:
      test: ["CMD-SHELL", "pg_isready -U postgres -d cybersec"]
      interval: 10s
      timeout: 5s
      retries: 5
    restart: unless-stopped

networks:
  external:
    driver: bridge
  internal:
    driver: bridge
    internal: true   # no route to Internet

volumes:
  pg-data:

secrets:
  pg_password:
    file: ./secrets/pg_password.txt
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 14: GitHub Actions Workflow}

The following GitHub Actions workflow implements the eight-stage DevSecOps pipeline. The workflow runs on every pull request and on every merge to \texttt{main}, with a required status check on the \texttt{sign} job.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=yaml]
name: DevSecOps Pipeline

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  pull_request:

permissions:
  contents: read
  packages: write
  id-token: write   # required for cosign keyless

jobs:
  sast:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: vimeo/psalm-action@v1
        with: { composer_require: true }
      - uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with: { python-version: "3.12" }
      - run: pip install bandit && bandit -r microservices/ -ll

  sca:
    needs: sast
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
        with: { scan-type: fs, severity: CRITICAL,HIGH }

  sbom:
    needs: sca
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: anchore/sbom-action@v0
        with: { format: cyclonedx-json, output-file: sbom.json }
      - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
        with: { name: sbom, path: sbom.json }

  build:
    needs: sbom
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4
      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v5
        with: { push: false, tags: cybersec:ci }

  scan-image:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: aquasecurity/trivy-action@master
        with: { image-ref: cybersec:ci, severity: CRITICAL,HIGH }

  sign:
    needs: scan-image
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: sigstore/cosign-installer@v3
      - run: cosign sign --yes ghcr.io/aymenazizi/cybersec:ci
\end{lstlisting}

\section*{Annex 15: Cytoscape.js Knowledge Graph Frontend}

The following Cytoscape.js initialisation code renders the knowledge graph in the browser. The graph is fetched from the Laravel endpoint \texttt{/projects/\{id\}/graph/data} as a JSON object with \texttt{nodes} and \texttt{edges} arrays.

\begin{lstlisting}[style=js]
const cy = cytoscape({
  container: document.getElementById("graph"),
  elements: await fetch(`/projects/${projectId}/graph/data`)
              .then(r => r.json()),
  style: [
    { selector: "node", style: {
        "background-color": "#7c3aed",
        "label": "data(label)",
        "color": "#fff",
        "text-valign": "center",
        "text-halign": "center",
        "font-size": "10px" } },
    { selector: 'node[type="vulnerability"]', style: {
        "background-color": "#ef4444" } },
    { selector: 'node[type="impact"]', style: {
        "background-color": "#f59e0b",
        "border-width": 2,
        "border-color": "#fbbf24" } },
    { selector: "edge", style: {
        "width": 1,
        "line-color": "#64748b",
        "target-arrow-color": "#64748b",
        "target-arrow-shape": "triangle",
        "curve-style": "bezier" } },
    { selector: ".highlighted", style: {
        "background-color": "#22c55e",
        "line-color": "#22c55e",
        "target-arrow-color": "#22c55e",
        "width": 3 } }
  ],
  layout: { name: "cose", animate: true, animationDuration: 600 }
});

// On node tap: compute blast-radius via BFS, highlight reachable nodes
cy.on("tap", "node", async evt => {
  const id = evt.target.id();
  const reachable = await fetch(`/assets/${id}/impact`)
                      .then(r => r.json());
  cy.elements().removeClass("highlighted");
  cy.getElementById(reachable.nodes).addClass("highlighted");
  reachable.edges.forEach(e =>
    cy.getElementById(e).addClass("highlighted"));
});
\end{lstlisting}
